<p><strong>Salon management software runs the daily work of a salon in one system: online bookings by stylist, staff schedules, payments, discounts and reports. For salons with more than one location it also shows every site in one place. Zennly is salon management software built for multi-branch salons.</strong></p>

<h2>What salon management software does</h2>
<p>It replaces the paper appointment book, phone calls and chat messages with one calendar. Clients choose a service, a stylist and a time, the slot is held, and the front desk, managers and owner all see the same day. Payments and discounts are recorded against each booking, so the end of the day is a check, not a search.</p>

<h2>Features worth looking for</h2>
<p>Not every salon needs everything, but these decide how well the software holds up as you grow:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Online booking by stylist and service, so clients book themselves without calling</li>
  <li>Clash prevention, so the same stylist cannot be booked twice at once</li>
  <li>Staff scheduling, including stylists who work at more than one location</li>
  <li>Payments recorded by method (cash, card, online) against each booking</li>
  <li>Discounts that need a manager's approval, with a record of who approved them</li>
  <li>Reports for revenue, staff performance and discounts</li>
  <li>Role-based access, so front-desk staff, managers and owners see what they need</li>
</ul>

<h2>One salon or several?</h2>
<p>A single salon can run on a simple calendar. Once you have a second location, the problems change: branches promise the same stylist, prices drift, discounts go unrecorded and the owner cannot see yesterday's numbers without phoning each manager. If you plan to grow, choose software that handles multiple locations from the start. See our guide to <a href="/blog/multi-location-booking-software/">multi-location booking software</a>.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes when choosing salon software</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Choosing on price alone, without checking what changes when you add a stylist or a location</li>
  <li>Not testing the demo with your own services, durations and prices</li>
  <li>Buying for one chair today and outgrowing it when a second location opens</li>
  <li>Skipping a check on how you can export your data later</li>
  <li>Rolling it out without a day of practice for the front-desk team</li>
</ul>
<p>Most of these cost more later than the licence costs now. A short trial with real bookings shows problems quickly.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before you buy</h2>
<p>Ask whether there is a setup fee, how the price changes when you add a stylist or a location, whether you can export your data, what support is included, and how the software handles payments and taxes in your country. Ask for a demo using your own services and prices.</p>

<h2>Salon, hair salon or beauty parlour?</h2>
<p>These are the same kind of business. 'Salon' is the common word in the USA, UK, Canada, Australia and the UAE, while 'beauty parlour' is common in Nepal and South Asia. The software works the same way.</p>

<h2>Where Zennly fits</h2>
<p>Zennly is built for salons with several branches that need one booking calendar, staff transfers between locations, discount approval and a daily close. If you only need a simple calendar for one chair, a lighter tool may be enough. See <a href="/solutions/salon/">how Zennly works for salons</a>.</p>
